---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Sample = `let steps = [8200, 4500, 8000, 8200, 3000, 12500, 7600];
let goal = 8000;

function daysAtGoal(log, target) {
  let count = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < log.length; i++) {
    if (log[i] >= target) {
      count = count + 1;
    }
  }

  return count;
}

let hit = daysAtGoal(steps, goal);
console.log("You hit your goal on " + hit + " of " + steps.length + " days.");`;

const PromptA = `WRITTEN RESPONSE 2(a) — Algorithm Development

Using the first iteration statement (loop) in your
Procedure segment:

  • how many times does its body execute?
  AND
  • describe a condition or change that would make
    it loop forever.

Both parts, or the point is not earned.`;

const PromptB = `WRITTEN RESPONSE 2(b) — Errors and Testing

Using the procedure in part (i) of your Procedure
segment:

  • describe a change to it that would cause a
    RUN-TIME error
  AND
  • explain why that change causes a run-time error.

Both parts, or the point is not earned.`;

const WorkedA = `The first loop is:

    for (let i = 0; i < log.length; i++)

log has 7 elements, so the body runs 7 TIMES.

To make it loop forever: remove  i++  (or change it
to  i = i). Then i never reaches log.length, the
condition i < log.length stays true, and the loop
never stops.`;

const WorkedB = `Change  return count  to  return counts

'counts' was never declared. When the procedure runs
and reaches that line, the program stops with a
RUN-TIME error:

    ReferenceError: counts is not defined

It is a run-time error, not a logic error, because the
program does not finish and produce a wrong answer —
it halts.`;

const Trap = `THE ANSWER THAT LOOKS RIGHT AND SCORES ZERO

  "Change  >=  to  >  and it gives the wrong count."

That is a LOGIC error. The program still runs and still
finishes. WR2(b) asks for a change that STOPS the
program. If it produces an answer at all, it is wrong
for this prompt.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 4.1: Written Responses 2(a) and 2(b)</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>Two of Your Six Points</h2>

          <p>
            <b>In May you answer four written-response prompts about your own
            program, from memory, in sixty minutes.</b> Two of them are today, and
            both are about your procedure and the loop inside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">These are worth two of the six points on the
            entire Create Task.</b> You cannot earn them by writing a better program
            now &mdash; your program is submitted. You earn them by being able to talk
            about it precisely.
          </p>

          <p>
            You rehearsed both of these in Quarter 3 &mdash; {" "}
            <code>wr-loop</code> was WR2(a) and <code>wr-runtime</code> was WR2(b) &mdash;
            against somebody else's code. <b>Today you do them against yours.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 4.1</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Written Responses 2(a) and 2(b)</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Both Halves, or Nothing</h2>
              <p>Every written-response point has the word <b>AND</b> in it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">WR2(a): a number <b>and</b> an infinite-loop change.</p>
              <p class="fragment">WR2(b): a change <b>and</b> why it errors.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Half an answer scores the same as a blank page.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Program to Practice On</h2>
              <p>Not yours &mdash; a fictional one, so we all read the same code.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Then you will do the same two responses about your own.</p>
              <Code code={Sample} frame="code" lang="javascript" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>WR2(a): Find the First Loop</h2>
              <p>Not any loop. The <b>first iteration statement</b> in your Procedure segment.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Count how many times its body runs.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Then break it on purpose &mdash; in words.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>WR2(b): Break It a Different Way</h2>
              <p>A change that <b>stops the program</b>. A run-time error.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Not a change that gives a wrong answer &mdash; that is a logic error.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The exam wants the program to <b>halt</b>, and it wants to know why.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Difference Decides the Point</h2>
              <p><b>Run-time error:</b> the program stops while running.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Logic error:</b> the program finishes and lies.</p>
              <p class="fragment">WR2(b) only accepts the first kind.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Identifying the first iteration statement in your own procedure</li>
                <li>Stating how many times its body executes, and what would make it loop forever</li>
                <li>Describing a change that produces a run-time error, and why</li>
                <li>Telling a run-time error from a logic error</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Worked: WR2(a) -->
        <section id="wr2a">
          <h2>Worked Example &mdash; WR2(a)</h2>

          <p>The fictional program:</p>
          <Code code={Sample} frame="code" lang="javascript" class="mb-4" />

          <Code code={PromptA} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Prompt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <p>A full-credit answer:</p>
          <Code code={WorkedA} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Both Halves" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Why this earns the point</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>It gives a <b>number</b> &mdash; 7 &mdash; and says <b>where it comes from</b> (the list length).</li>
            <li>It names a <b>specific change</b> and explains why the loop never ends.</li>
            <li>Both halves are present. <b>That is the whole test.</b></li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The most common way to lose this point is to
            answer only the first half</b> &mdash; give the count and stop. The number is
            necessary and it is not sufficient.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Worked: WR2(b) -->
        <section id="wr2b">
          <h2>Worked Example &mdash; WR2(b)</h2>

          <Code code={PromptB} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Prompt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-4" />

          <p>A full-credit answer, about the same procedure:</p>
          <Code code={WorkedB} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Both Halves" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <h5 class="mt-4">And the answer that feels right and is not</h5>
          <Code code={Trap} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Read This Twice" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>You met this exact trap at <code>wr-runtime</code>.</b> The test is one
            question: <b>does the program stop, or does it finish with a wrong
            answer?</b> WR2(b) wants the one that stops.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Apply -->
        <section id="apply">
          <h2>Now Your Own Program</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Open your Personalized Project Reference.</b> Look at the procedure in
            part (i) and the first loop inside it. Answer both prompts about it, in
            full sentences, on paper.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Your WR2(a)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write out the first loop's header from your procedure.</li>
            <li><b>How many times does its body run?</b> If it depends on a list, say &quot;once for each element of &lt;list name&gt;&quot; &mdash; that is a correct answer.</li>
            <li>Describe one change that would make it loop forever, and say <b>why</b> it never stops.</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Your WR2(b)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Describe one change to that procedure that causes a <b>run-time error</b>.</li>
            <li>Explain <b>why</b> it errors &mdash; what the program tries to do that it cannot.</li>
            <li>Check your answer against the test: <b>does the program stop?</b> If it would still finish, you have described a logic error &mdash; try again.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">If your procedure has no loop in it, tell me
            now.</b> WR2(a) asks about a loop in your Procedure segment. This is the
            last place that can be sorted out before the exam, and it matters more
            than anything else on this page.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>What are the two halves of a WR2(a) answer?</li>
            <li>What is the one-question test that separates a run-time error from a logic error?</li>
            <li>Which of the two prompts is about a loop, and which is about the whole procedure?</li>
            <li>Can you answer WR2(a) about your own procedure right now, both halves?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Your WR2(a) and WR2(b), in full sentences, about your own program.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
